five-month-old sadie hasn't had her  first step or  first tooth yet her parents have already  taken hundreds of photos  keen to capture every moment she hates  it but the pandemic they say has robbed  them of one of life's most  precious experiences as a family i'd  been in there for three days with no  sleep  and then i was kind of left on my own  after a c-section i couldn't sit up and  i'm suddenly responsible for another  life with absolutely no  no help throughout the pandemic the nhs  didn't allow  birth partners to be in the room until  mothers were four centimeters dilated  in christie's case that led to more  anxiety  as a new mum christie says it's left her  feeling sad  about the experiences of giving birth  it's a time that can be  frightening um it's a time when you know  somebody's in a lot of pain potentially  and things can change somebody might end  up needing  an emergency cesarean these are all  stressful life events but social support  can buffer life events with 1800 babies  born  every day during the pandemic thousands  of other parents would have faced  similar separations  all aimed at reducing the spread of  covid19  jordan and lucy's pregnancy ended in a  miscarriage  a difficult time to have been apart  throughout the course of  the time since finding out that we were  miscarrying the only thing that  had given me any sense of like  directional purpose was that i just  wanted to like be there with you  and i didn't want you to be on your own  so as soon as i realized that  you were going through that alone and i  couldn't do anything at all about it  it just kind of hit me very hard it kind  of might rock but like  um i was in the room by myself  for seven hours and  yeah it was just awful today the  government called on maternity centers  to try harder to get the balance right  i think it's vital that all local  hospital trusts and local health bodies  who have the  responsibility for administering the  rules in their particular  area really lean into this issue i would  like to see  more trusts get that balance right to  allow loved ones in particularly  when difficult and tough news has to be  delivered  the nhs told us while it is  understandable that the pandemic has  caused expectant mums  increased anxiety the important changes  to maternity services  backed by the royal college of midwives  were made to keep women  and their families safe now we have  moved past the peak of the pandemic and  after extensive engagement with the  various professional bodies involved  new guidance has been issued to all  maternity units  so partners can attend antenatal and  postnatal clinics safely  in addition to attending labour which  they have been able to do  throughout the pandemic so this week  maternity services can start to find  ways to reintroduce partners  under these new guidelines baby steps  towards getting delivery suites  back to normal sadia chowdhury sky news
 

